WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.767
Structured notes turn browsing into reviewable work.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:09.750
Begin each case with the requirement, scope, authority, deadlines, and handling rules.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:17.506
Use dated entries that distinguish search actions, direct observations, source claims, questions, assumptions, and analytical judgments.

00:00:17.506 --> 00:00:29.250
A source log should record a stable source identifier, URL, page title, publisher or account, publication date when available, access timestamp, relevance, and preservation reference.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:34.790
This allows citations and later review even if the page changes.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:40.330
Store approved screenshots, archives, and downloaded public documents with consistent names.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:45.870
A useful convention may include date, source, short subject, and identifier.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:52.296
Avoid names such as screenshot one that lose meaning outside the analyst's desktop.

00:00:52.296 --> 00:00:56.949
Separate raw collection from working material and final products.

00:00:56.949 --> 00:01:07.807
Preserve originals when integrity matters, record transformations such as translation or cropping, and link summaries or extracted facts back to the source identifier.

00:01:07.807 --> 00:01:13.790
Folder structures should be simple enough that another analyst can navigate them.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:20.660
Typical areas include administration, source logs, raw collection, working notes, analysis, reports, and exports.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.313
Access permissions and retention may differ across these areas.

00:01:25.313 --> 00:01:27.750
Case organization protects reasoning.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:37.722
It lets reviewers see whether a judgment rests on independent evidence or repeated copies, whether dates align, and where uncertainty remains.

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:44.591
Good structure is not clerical overhead; it is part of analytical quality and accountability.
